.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Legacy locations, all four of them. Everything the app generates lives under
# ~/.warlock now, and a checkout carrying these has them moved there on the next
# start (warlock/migrate.py) -- but the entries stay, because they are here
# until that has run and because WARLOCK_HOME can still point back at the
# checkout deliberately.
#
# Root-anchored, as /bench/ and /vendor/ are: unanchored, any future package
# directory with one of these names (say src/warlock/studio/assets/) would
# silently vanish from the index.
/assets/
# Benchmark runs: generated artifacts, copied rather than referenced, and
# reclaimed with `python -m warlock.bench prune`. The suites and recipes that
# define a run live in src/ and are tracked.
/bench/
/models/
/palettes/
/vendor/
# Copies of assets/jobs.sqlite taken before a campaign submit. The job database
# is under /assets/ and therefore untracked; a backup of it is no more tracked
# than the original, and a sweep campaign takes one per run.
/backups/
# Intermediate objects from native/build.ps1. The DLL it produces lands in
# /vendor/ with the other native binaries; this is just the scratch beside it.
/native/obj/
/scratch/
# `warlock sweep` output: measurement GLBs and logs, regenerable and large.
/sweep/
.idea/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
dist/
/build/
*.egg-info/
# Claude Code's per-machine state stays out, but the skills are project
# knowledge (/exercise-mode, /warlock-audit) and travel with the tree. Git
# cannot re-include a file under an excluded directory, so the parent is
# matched with a glob and the one subdirectory negated.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/
# imgui's window layout, rewritten every time the app is closed. Tracking it
# meant every run produced a diff and every branch produced a conflict, over a
# file that is per-machine by definition.
imgui.ini
# Measurement corpora: the images a docs/measurements/ document was eyeballed
# against. Regenerable from the scripts/ harness that produced them (fixed
# seeds), and 49 MB for the seam corpus alone. The results JSON beside them is
# the evidence and is tracked.
/docs/measurements/data/**/*.png
# And the meshes, for the same reason and more so: a re-texture corpus is a
# copy of the input GLB plus its re-textured output, which is 27 MB a pair.
/docs/measurements/data/**/*.glb
# Screenshot-harness output (`scripts/screenshot_modes.py --out`), and the
# `WARLOCK_HOME` each run is pointed at. Per-run, regenerable, photographed for
# one review -- never an input to anything, and tens of MB of PNGs a pass.
#
# **One pattern, not one line per review.** This had grown a fourth entry
# (`.tmp-ui-audit`, `.tmp-plotter-review*`, `.tmp-studio-audit`, and the Inker
# UX pass) because each reviewer picked a new directory name, and the line
# after the one they read did not cover them. A `git add -A` after a harness
# run commits whatever the newest name was, so the rule is the prefix.
/.tmp-*/

# Sample inputs, deliberately untracked as of 2026-08-24 and **not** merely for
# size. Three of these are third-party art this project has no right to
# redistribute: `light_world.png` and `zelda_1.jpg` are Nintendo-derived (a rip
# of A Link to the Past and a screenshot beside it, kept only as look
# references and colour-destroyed for that purpose), and the `*_base.obj`,
# `*_base.blend` and `*_spritesheet.png` files are ULPC-derived under
# CC-BY-SA/GPL with no attribution travelling beside them.
#
# All 20 were git-tracked, so making the repository public would have published
# them -- and hatchling's sdist default swept the whole ~26 MB into every
# `uv build`, which CI runs on every push. `pyproject.toml`'s
# `[tool.hatch.build.targets.sdist]` allowlist is the other half of this;
# `tests/test_release_hygiene.py` pins both.
#
# The history purge completed on 2026-09-03: the blobs were stripped from every
# earlier commit and the remote recreated, so nothing reachable carries them any
# more. `/examples/` stays untracked regardless, because the underlying assets
# are third-party and unlicensed for redistribution.
/examples/
